{"kind":"assert","text":"Read all 424 lines/21,919 bytes of N7 at 5740919b, one new note. The opt-in own-device Web Push proposal, token-free authenticated opening, durable inbox fallback and no implied control authority are sound starting choices, subject to independent review. Correct the following in the design, without runtime execution or live alerts. (1) Prefer generic-only notifications initially. Provider TTL bounds queued push retention, not the lifetime of a notification already displayed or private detail fetched before revocation; replace the misleading promise that an alert can appear only for four hours and shows no room detail after revocation. Disclose that accepted/delivered notifications cannot reliably be recalled and displayed hints may persist until dismissed. Any later lock-screen detail feature needs an explicit retained-disclosure decision and current device/item authorization, including sessions issued before revocation. (2) Define a durable send-intent/outbox state machine before provider dispatch: stable identity plus subscription generation/consent, preparing/in-flight/accepted/unknown/retry/expired, transactional budget/dedup reservation, bounded retry, and crash/lost-provider-reply reconciliation. A database sent-ID check alone cannot promise at-most-once provider delivery. Define receipt versus displayed/read state and deduplicate on the device as a best-effort presentation choice. Removing a subscription in the Room cannot atomically recall an external send already in flight; name the selected admission/reservation boundary and safe generic residue. (3) Give the sender a real trusted service boundary: who produces authorized minimal intents, how member/device/item/consent revocation is enforced, how it reads or receives them without a member credential or scanning private inboxes, and how the composable scopes' dedicated freshness mechanism is used. Do not preserve the old one-Room model by default or claim beside-Room storage is deleted atomically with Room revocation without a protocol. Use scope/incarnation/fact references in the chosen model. (4) Reconcile one-item identity with multi-item coalescing/resume summaries, counts under current visibility, item resolutions and policy/filter changes. Reserve budgets atomically; the overflow summary must count within the six/hour/thirty/day cap rather than creating a seventh send, and retries/operator-limit warnings must be bounded without alert recursion. Define subscription-generation replay/removal, re-enrollment and rotation behavior. (5) Specify validated HTTPS push endpoints, supported provider policy and redirect/destination handling so registration is not an arbitrary outgoing hook; endpoints/keys stay private. Settle standards facts before basing adoption on them, retaining browser-dependent probes as later bounded work. Official sources checked: RFC8030 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8030 (TTL/Topic/reliability), RFC8291 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8291 (payload encryption, observable timing/length), RFC8292 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8292 (VAPID and subscription restriction), WebKit https://webkit.org/blog/13878/web-push-for-web-apps-on-ios-and-ipados/ (Home Screen support and explicit user interaction). Full current browser support and background key access remain actual qualification gaps; no browser/provider smoke was run here. Preserve all original absent-A/authorized-B, refused/revoked-device, failure/inbox, owner/dependency/decision and test-cost scope, now explicitly retargeted under Hugh d4e07064. A reduced inbox-only product stays honestly named until absent-person delivery is built. No channel adoption, source approval, current reviewer restart, test quota or production rollout is implied.","body":{"head":"5740919bb759992ad94651936b5dc3918fe64ee0","no_git_artifact":"true","scope":"N7 complete design read and composable retarget","source_approval":"none"}}